  "narrative": {
    "text": "Figure 3.4.2, 'A terminology maturity spectrum, from minimal reference-list management to a fully governed, multi-stakeholder ecosystem', adapted from the OpenHIE Terminology Service maturity model (section 3.4.5). A rising line plots five maturity levels: Nascent (no centrally defined content or governance); Emerging (some centrally defined content in non-standard formats, with limited adoption and governance); Shared Electronic Reference (up-to-date definitions aligned across key programmes); Digital Subscription (key systems subscribe electronically to the central service to stay up to date); and Data Exchange (the terminology service automates information exchange, for example code validation and value set expansion). Red break marks between Emerging and Shared Electronic Reference show a steep climb. A callout notes that many LMICs are at early stages and that this step needs a different way of doing business. Braces underneath group the first three levels as emphasising selection, definition and governance of standards, and the last two as emphasising use of terminologies for data exchange.",
